  "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $7-\\sqrt{x-5}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $7-\\sqrt{x-5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{7+\\sqrt{x-5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{7+\\sqrt{x-5}}\\cdot \\frac{7-\\sqrt{x-5}}{7-\\sqrt{x-5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(7-\\sqrt{x-5})}{(7+\\sqrt{x-5})(7-\\sqrt{x-5})} & = \\\\ \\frac{7x-x\\sqrt{x-5}}{{7}^{2}-(\\sqrt{x-5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7x-x\\sqrt{x-5}}{49-(x-5)} & = \\\\ \\frac{7x-x\\sqrt{x-5}}{54-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
